  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday) \nIssued at 407 PM EDT SUN AUG 30 2026\n\nThe main story of the long term period will be heat. On Tuesday \nmorning, there is high confidence that an anomalously strong upper \nlevel ridge will sit directly over Kentucky. LREF mean 500-mb \nheights reach 595 dam across the forecast area, representing the \n99th percentile when compared to climatology, with minimal spread \namong ensemble solutions. Subsidence associated with this ridge \nshould suppress convection, keeping weather dry and PoPs near zero \nthrough at least midweek. Meanwhile, at the surface, high pressure \nover the Southern Appalachians will keep winds out of the southwest \nacross the forecast area, maintaining a moist air mass across \neastern Kentucky. There is still some uncertainty regarding how high \ndewpoints will climb, owing to model disagreement regarding the \nexact positioning of the surface high (and associated wind pattern); \nhowever, ensemble guidance indicates dewpoints in the mid to upper \n60s are likely, with raw NBM output even suggesting widespread \ndewpoints in the 70s (with the highest dewpoints in our southwestern \ncounties). Given that both ensemble and deterministic guidance favor \na drier solution, these NBM dewpoints are likely overdone, and \nforecast dewpoints were nudged down a few degrees to better match \nmodel consensus at this time. Should this hold, we will narrowly \navoid hitting heat advisory criteria, though apparent temperatures \ncould reach the triple digits. Regardless, expect hot and humid \nweather on Tuesday and Wednesday. High temperatures will generally \nrange from the mid to upper 90s, with the hottest temperatures \ncloser to the I-64 corridor. Overnight, limited relief is expected; \nlows on Tuesday and Wednesday nights will range from the upper 60s \nin sheltered valley locations to the low 70s on ridges, with typical \nvalley fog formation likely. \n\nA pattern change is expected on Thursday as upper level troughing \nover the Northeast digs southward, flattening and weakening the \nridge over Kentucky. There is still some disagreement over how \nquickly this ridging will break down over us; however, high \ntemperatures should cool from Thursday onward, and rain chances look \nto increase through the end of the period. With a trend toward \nnorthwesterly flow, pattern recognition suggests that this setup \ncould open the door for multiple shortwave impulses to ride the \nperiphery of the ridge. In fact, deterministic guidance shows a \nsignal for MCS type activity to develop in the Midwest and progress \nsoutheastward with time, potentially into our area. That being said, \nthe track of any convective complexes would depend on the position \nand strength of upper level ridging. Unfortunately, models disagree \nsignificantly on these details on Thursday and Friday; the ECMWF \nfavors a more quasi-zonal pattern aloft during this time, while the \nGFS keeps flow more northwesterly, with a much more amplified ridge \nto the west over the Mississippi Valley. In both scenarios, some \nscattered showers and storms would be possible on Friday, but \nconfidence in any further forecast details remains very low at this \ntime. \n\nBy the weekend, models come into better agreement regarding a more \nrobust northwest flow pattern aloft as ridging continues to amplify \nover the Plains. Meanwhile, at the surface, a weak cold front will \nsag south into the forecast area, providing additional forcing for \nmore focused shower and storm chances, although details again remain \nquite uncertain at this stage in the forecast. Thankfully, \ntemperatures will fall closer to normal around this time, bringing \nan end to any heat concerns.",
